    "section": "reading",
    "skill": "Main idea",
    "q": "Which conclusion best captures the passage as a whole?",
    "o": [
      "A suitable map preserves the distinctions needed for its intended task.",
      "More shoreline detail always makes a walking map more useful.",
      "The survey plan is less accurate because it is harder to read while walking.",
      "The oval proves that the pond's real shore is regular."
    ],
    "e": "Jawaban sesuai makna dan batas informasi yang dijelaskan berikut.",
    "origin": "original",
    "passage": {
      "id": "SM-LV-8f4545539544",
      "title": "Shadows on a map",
      "text": "A walking map represents connections between paths, not every object beside them. Its scale deliberately suppresses small features that do not help a visitor choose a route.\n\nA pond may therefore appear as a simple oval even when its shore is irregular. This simplification is useful until a traveller needs to locate a narrow landing point on that shore.\n\nA survey plan records the missing shoreline detail but is harder to read while walking. The two drawings serve different decisions rather than competing to reproduce everything equally well.\n\nA suitable map preserves the distinctions needed for its intended task. Adding detail can reduce usefulness when it obscures the junctions that matter."
    },
    "solution": {
      "steps": [
        "The final paragraph draws the conclusion from the preceding distinction. The alternatives each collapse a distinction made in the case.",
        "A suitable map preserves the distinctions needed for its intended task. Adding detail can reduce usefulness when it obscures the junctions that matter."
      ],
      "evidence": "A suitable map preserves the distinctions needed for its intended task."
    },
    "difficulty": "standard",
