      "title": "Two catalogues",
      "text": "One archive catalogue groups letters by the sender, while another groups them by the event discussed. Neither arrangement changes the letters themselves.\n\nA biographer can follow a person's correspondence easily in the first catalogue. A researcher tracing a flood must instead combine entries from many senders.\n\nThe event catalogue reverses that advantage. Its cross-references lead back to the sender catalogue when a question concerns both a person and an event.\n\nEfficiency thus depends on the question being asked. Measuring only biography searches would favour one arrangement before the comparison even began."
